WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.687
Reallocation combines memory ownership,
size arithmetic, and failure handling.

2
00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:07.877
The realloc function can resize memory,
move memory, or fail.

3
00:00:07.957 --> 00:00:11.964
A resize routine should be written so the
object remains valid no matter which of

4
00:00:12.044 --> 00:00:13.240
those outcomes occurs.

5
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.627
Assigning realloc directly to the original
pointer can leak the original allocation

6
00:00:17.707 --> 00:00:18.305
on failure.

7
00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:22.572
If realloc fails, the original allocation
remains valid and still needs to be owned

8
00:00:22.652 --> 00:00:23.369
and released.

9
00:00:23.449 --> 00:00:27.576
Overwriting the only owner pointer with a
null failure result loses the ability to

10
00:00:27.656 --> 00:00:29.451
clean up that original allocation.

11
00:00:29.531 --> 00:00:33.658
A temporary pointer helps preserve
ownership until resize success is confirmed.

12
00:00:33.738 --> 00:00:36.848
Store the result of realloc in a temporary
variable, check it,

13
00:00:36.928 --> 00:00:38.901
and only then update the owner pointer.

14
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:43.288
This pattern keeps the old allocation
reachable when the resize cannot be completed.

15
00:00:43.368 --> 00:00:46.777
Count and capacity should be updated only
after successful resize.

16
00:00:46.857 --> 00:00:50.804
Updating metadata too early can leave a
buffer claiming space it does not have,

17
00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:54.234
or cause cleanup code to believe the
object changed when it did not.

18
00:00:54.314 --> 00:00:58.501
A failed resize should leave the old
object state intact and usable according to the

19
00:00:58.581 --> 00:00:59.119
contract.

20
00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:03.206
Allocation size calculations should
account for overflow before allocating.

21
00:01:03.286 --> 00:01:07.294
Capacity growth often involves
multiplication, addition, terminator space,

22
00:01:07.374 --> 00:01:08.570
or structure overhead.

23
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:10.683
If the computed size wraps or truncates,

24
00:01:10.763 --> 00:01:13.993
the allocation request may not represent
the intended storage.

25
00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:16.525
Check arithmetic before calling the
allocator.

26
00:01:16.605 --> 00:01:20.613
Resize logic should preserve a valid
object state whether it succeeds or fails.

27
00:01:20.693 --> 00:01:23.683
The caller should know whether old data
remains available,

28
00:01:23.763 --> 00:01:27.352
whether the buffer changed, and what
cleanup responsibility remains.

29
00:01:27.432 --> 00:01:31.140
Clear resize contracts prevent leaks from
hiding inside error handling.
